<!--
  Attack Simulation & Detection Engineering Lab - T1053 / T1547 (Persistence)
  Catches a cron job added to the crontab spool on Linux (via Wazuh File
  Integrity Monitoring) and a registry Run-key addition on Windows (via
  Sysmon Event ID 13), as two rules in the same group.

  The Linux <if_sid> parent below is Wazuh's well-known default rule ID
  (550/553 for syscheck file-modified events).

  The Windows rule (100211) went through three real fixes, in order:
  1. It originally guessed <if_group>sysmon_event13</if_group> (no
     underscore), which doesn't exist - Wazuh's own upstream ruleset
     (0330-sysmon_rules.xml) is inconsistent here: events 1-9 register
     a group with no underscore (sysmon_event1...sysmon_event9), but
     10 and up switch to one (sysmon_event_10, sysmon_event_11,
     sysmon_event_12, sysmon_event_13...). Confirmed via the manager
     log warning `Group 'sysmon_event13' was not found. Invalid
     'if_group'. Rule '100211' will be ignored.` on every restart -
     first fixed by switching to the real group name, sysmon_event_13.
  2. Once real Sysmon Event ID 13 telemetry from dc01-lab was actually
     flowing into Wazuh's archives (getting to that point surfaced a
     separate, unrelated agent-enrollment bug - a stale duplicate
     agent registration under the same hostname that had to be
     removed on the manager before the agent could reconnect), running
     that real archived event through wazuh-logtest showed the
     if_group approach still wasn't the most direct match. The rule
     was switched to match directly on the decoded fields
     (win.system.providerName, win.system.eventID,
     win.eventdata.targetObject) instead of chaining through the
     group.
  3. Even with real telemetry flowing and the rule loading cleanly, a
     live reg.exe run key simulation never produced a 100211 alert.
     The real cause: this manager already had a community Sysmon
     detection pack installed (rule 92302, groups
     sysmon,sysmon_eid13_detections,windows - already MITRE-mapped to
     T1547.001), from the portfolio's separate Windows/Sysmon lab.
     Since 100211 was written as an independent top-level rule (no
     parent), and Wazuh's engine commits to the first matching
     top-level rule per event, 92302 claimed every Run-key event
     before 100211 was ever evaluated - confirmed directly by finding
     the real alert in archives.json filed under rule 92302, not
     100211. Fixed by chaining 100211 as a child of 92302 via
     <if_sid>, so it only evaluates (and, being more specific, wins)
     once the parent has already matched - the standard Wazuh
     escalation pattern, rather than two competing independent rules.
-->
<group name="attack_sim,persistence,t1053,t1547,">

  <!-- Linux: cron spool file modified (FIM) -->
  <rule id="100210" level="12">
    <if_sid>550,553</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)(cron\.d|crontabs)</field>
    <description>Persistence: cron job spool modified</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.003</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Windows: registry Run key value set - chained as a child of the
       pre-existing community rule 92302 (which was silently winning
       the match ahead of an independent 100211), confirmed via a real
       archived Sysmon Event ID 13 from dc01-lab filed under 92302 -->
  <rule id="100211" level="12">
    <if_sid>92302</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)CurrentVersion.*Run</field>
    <description>Persistence: registry Run key value added</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
